  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon shows a large area of high \npressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic states with southerly flow \nstretched from the southern Plains into the Upper Midwest. \nTemperatures will remain unseasonably warm through midweek for most, \nwith highs peaking in the upper 70s to low 80s areawide this \nafternoon, then topping out in the upper 70s to 80s near/south of I-\n72 Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nTropical moisture stemming from Hurricane Polo will get lifted \nnorthward through the central US then spill into the middle \nMississippi Valley beginning tonight, bringing likely chances for \nrain showers to areas north of a Quincy to Bloomington line through \nWednesday afternoon. More widespread shower and occasional storm \nchances will impact the entire area Thursday into Friday as a cold \nfront pushes the tropical moisture further southeast through the \nregion. Climatologically high PWATS (up around 2 inches) and warm \ncloud processes will support pockets of heavy rain, with areas near \nand west of the Illinois River expected to receive the highest \namounts. The National Blend of Models (NBM) shows a 50-90% chance of \nexceeding 1 inch of rain north of I-70 through Friday with a 50-70% \nchance of exceeding 2 inches north of a Jacksonville to Bloomington \nline. This may lead to localized flooding and rises on area rivers, \nstreams, and creeks where the heaviest rain occurs.\n\nHigh pressure settles into the Midwest states behind the cold front \nlater Friday into Saturday, bringing a period of more seasonable \ntemperatures and drier weather through at least early next \nweek.",
